    "result": "I've successfully fixed the tax rounding issue in the codebase. Here's what I accomplished:\n\n## Problem Identified\nThe tax calculation was using Python's built-in `round()` function which implements \"banker's rounding\" (round half to even), but the tests expected \"round half up\" behavior (round half away from zero).\n\n## Root Cause\nIn `invoicing/money.py`, the `round_half_up()` function was incorrectly using Python's built-in `round()` function instead of implementing proper half-up rounding.\n\n## Fix Applied\nI modified the `round_half_up()` function to properly implement \"round half up\" behavior using `math.floor()` and `math.ceil()` for correct rounding behavior.\n\n## Verification\n- \u2705 The failing test `test_reduced_rate_on_250_cents_rounds_up` now passes\n- \u2705 All 85 tests in the test suite pass\n- \u2705 No regressions introduced\n\nThe fix ensures that when calculating tax on exact half-cent amounts (like 5% of $2.50 = 12.5 cents), the system now correctly rounds up to 13 cents instead of rounding to the nearest even number (12 cents).\n\nThe change is minimal and focused, only affecting the rounding behavior without altering any other functionality. All existing tests now pass, confirming the fix resolves the issue without introducing new problems.",
